# Manage Docs & File Reading Protocol

## manage_docs: Create Is Not Enough

**`manage_docs(action="create")` scaffolds an EMPTY template. It is NOT a document.**

```
WRONG: manage_docs(action="create", ...) then treating the scaffold as the report → empty document
WRONG: open_bug(...) + manage_docs(action="create", ...) + nothing more → empty scaffold ≠ bug report
RIGHT: create → replace_section for each meaningful section → document now has content
```

### Required Pattern

```python
# 1. Scaffold — empty template
manage_docs(agent="<agent>", action="create", doc_name="RESEARCH_X",
    metadata={"doc_type": "research", "research_goal": "..."})

# 2. Write content — this is where the document actually exists
manage_docs(agent="<agent>", action="replace_section",
    doc_name="RESEARCH_X", section="findings", content="Actual findings...")
manage_docs(agent="<agent>", action="replace_section",
    doc_name="RESEARCH_X", section="recommendations", content="...")
```

## manage_docs: Prefer `apply_patch` for Edits

**`apply_patch` is the primary edit action.** It uses context matching so it handles document drift gracefully. `replace_range` uses bare line numbers which break when content shifts.

```
WRONG: replace_range with line numbers from a prior scan → lines shifted, wrong content replaced
RIGHT: apply_patch with unified diff → context lines anchor the edit regardless of line drift
```

```python
# Preferred: unified diff with context anchoring
manage_docs(agent="<agent>", action="apply_patch", doc_name="architecture",
    patch="--- before\n+++ after\n@@ -5,3 +5,4 @@\n existing line\n+new line\n context\n",
    dry_run=True)  # ALWAYS dry_run first

# Structured edit via apply_patch (section/block replacement)
manage_docs(agent="<agent>", action="apply_patch", doc_name="architecture",
    edit={"type": "replace_section", "section": "findings", "content": "Updated..."})
```

## manage_docs: Allowed Actions

Use stable document actions. Discovery and quality actions are allowed when they support safe document edits and handoff proof.

**WRITE / EDIT**: `create`, `replace_section`, `apply_patch`, `replace_range`, `replace_text`, `append`, `status_update`, `frontmatter_update`

**DISCOVERY / QUALITY**: `list_sections`, `list_checklist_items`, `search`, `quality_check`

**MAINTENANCE (use deliberately)**: `normalize_headers`, `generate_toc`, `validate_crosslinks`, `batch`

## Frontmatter Intent and Quality Gates

Narrative document frontmatter is not checklist progress.

```python
# Narrative-doc metadata/status belongs in frontmatter_update
manage_docs(agent="<agent>", action="frontmatter_update", doc_name="architecture",
    metadata={"frontmatter": {"status": "ready", "owners": ["blueprint"]}})

# Checklist proof belongs in status_update
manage_docs(agent="<agent>", action="status_update", doc_name="checklist",
    section="task_1", metadata={"status": "done", "proof": "pytest ... passed"})
```

If `status_update` is used on a narrative doc, treat `DOC_STATUS_INTENT_MISMATCH` as a real error and use `frontmatter_update`.

Before claiming a managed doc is ready, run:

```python
manage_docs(agent="<agent>", action="quality_check", doc_name="architecture", dry_run=True)
```

Treat `DOC_NOT_DONE_SCAFFOLD_QUALITY` and blocking `SCF_*` warnings as completion blockers. Scaffold residue, placeholder prose, empty findings, TODO-only ready sections, and frontmatter/body mismatches mean the artifact is not done.

## Frontmatter and Line Numbers

Every manage_docs response includes `document_info` showing the frontmatter layout:

```json
{"document_info": {"frontmatter_lines": 16, "body_lines": 45, "total_lines": 61, "body_starts_at_line": 17}}
```

**Why this matters:** Scribe documents have YAML frontmatter. Line numbers from `read_file` are file-relative (include frontmatter). The `replace_range` action via the MCP tool auto-adjusts file-relative numbers to body-relative. If you pass body-relative numbers directly, use `metadata={"line_reference": "body"}`.

## read_file: Scan Before You Read

**MANDATORY: Always scan first. Never read full content without scanning.**

```
WRONG: read_file(path="large_file.py", mode="full_stream") → wastes context without knowing what you need
RIGHT: scan_only first → get structure + line numbers → line_range for targeted reads
```

```python
# Step 1: Scan — get structure and line numbers, no content
read_file(agent="<agent>", path="src/module.py", mode="scan_only", include_dependencies=True)
# → Returns: class/function names, line numbers, imports

# Step 2: Read only what you need
read_file(agent="<agent>", path="src/module.py", mode="line_range", start_line=142, end_line=165)
```

| Mode | When | Cost |
|------|------|------|
| `scan_only` | Always first | Cheapest |
| `line_range` | After scan, targeted | Low |
| `search` | Find patterns in file | Low-Medium |
| `full_stream` | Small files only | Expensive |

## Section Naming

Use the section ID from the doc scaffold, not arbitrary strings:

```
WRONG: replace_section(section="my analysis notes") → orphaned section
RIGHT: replace_section(section="findings") → matches scaffold ID from <!-- ID: findings --> comment
```
